RDOMAIN(4)               BSD Kernel Interfaces Manual               RDOMAIN(4)

NNAAMMEE
     rrttaabbllee, rrddoommaaiinn — routing tables and routing domains

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The traditional kernel routing system had a single table for routes and
     allowed only non-conflicting IP address assignments.  The rrttaabbllee feature
     allows multiple lookup tables for routes.  The rrddoommaaiinn feature provides a
     way to logically segment a router between network paths.

   RRoouuttiinngg ttaabblleess
     Each rrttaabbllee contains routes for outbound network packets.  A routing do‐
     main can contain more than one rrttaabbllee.  Multiple routing tables are com‐
     monly used for Policy Based Routing.

     The highest ID that can be used for an rrttaabbllee is 255.

   RRoouuttiinngg ddoommaaiinnss
     Each rrddoommaaiinn is a completely separate address space in the kernel.  An IP
     address (e.g. 10.0.0.1/16) can be assigned in more than one rrddoommaaiinn, but
     cannot be assigned more than once per rrddoommaaiinn.  An interface belongs to
     one and only one rrddoommaaiinn.  The interface's rrddoommaaiinn determines which rdo‐
     main an incoming packet will be in.  Virtual interfaces do not need to
     belong to the same rrddoommaaiinn as the parent.  Each rrddoommaaiinn contains at least
     one routing table.

     Network traffic within an rrddoommaaiinn stays within the current routing do‐
     main.  pf(4) is used to move traffic from one rrddoommaaiinn to a different
     rrddoommaaiinn.

     When an interface is assigned to a non-existent rrddoommaaiinn, it gets created
     automatically.  At the same time an rrttaabbllee with the same ID and a lo(4)
     interface with a unit number matching the ID get created and assigned to
     the new domain.

     An rdomain can be deleted by removing all interfaces from it and then de‐
     stroying the lo(4) interface with the unit number matching the ID.

     The highest ID that can be used for an rrddoommaaiinn is 255.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Put em0 and lo4 in rdomain 4:

           # ifconfig em0 rdomain 4
           # ifconfig lo4 inet 127.0.0.1/8
           # ifconfig em0 192.0.2.100/24

     List all rdomains with associated interfaces and routing tables:

           $ netstat -R

     Set a default route and localhost reject route within rtable 4:

           # route -T4 -qn add -net 127 127.0.0.1 -reject
           # route -T4 -n add default 192.0.2.1

     Start sshd(8) in rtable 4:

           # route -T4 exec /usr/sbin/sshd

     Display the routing table used by each process:

           $ ps aux -o rtable

     Display the routing table of the current process:

           $ id -R

     A pf.conf(5) snippet to block incoming port 80, and nat-to and move to
     rtable 0 on interface em1:

           block in on rdomain 4 proto tcp to any port 80
           match out on rdomain 4 to !$internal_net nat-to (em1) rtable 0

     Delete rdomain 4 again:

           # ifconfig em0 -rdomain
           # ifconfig lo4 destroy

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     id(1), netstat(1), ps(1), lo(4), route(4), pf.conf(5), ifconfig(8),
     route(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     OpenBSD support for rrddoommaaiinn first appeared in OpenBSD 4.9 and IPv6 sup‐
     port first appeared in OpenBSD 5.5.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
     No tool is available to assign more than one rtable to an rdomain other
     than to the default one (0).

     An rtable cannot be deleted.  Deleting an rdomain will move its rtable
     into the default rdomain.

